[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) Internals"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to MVCC"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) is a concurrency control method commonly used by database management systems to provide concurrent access to the database and in programming languages to implement transactional memory. Traditional locking mechanisms can severely degrade performance under high read-write contention. MVCC solves this by maintaining multiple physical versions of a single logical record."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Mechanics: How MVCC Works"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Readers do not block writers, and writers do not block readers."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Updates create a new version of the data tuple rather than overwriting the existing data in-place."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deletes typically mark a tuple as logically deleted by setting a tombstone or an expiration transaction ID."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Tuple Header Structure"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To manage multiple versions, database engines augment every tuple with metadata fields in its header. For instance, in PostgreSQL, a tuple header contains hidden attributes such as xmin and xmax."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"struct HeapTupleHeaderData {
    TransactionId t_xmin; // Inserting transaction ID
    TransactionId t_xmax; // Deleting or updating transaction ID
    CommandId     t_cid;  // Command ID within the transaction
    // ... other flags and fields
};"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"xmin: Stores the ID of the transaction that created (inserted) this specific version of the tuple."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"xmax: Stores the ID of the transaction that deleted or updated (superseded) this version. If the tuple is active, xmax is typically zero."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Transaction Visibility Rules"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a transaction reads data, it evaluates the tuple headers against its own Snapshot. A snapshot typically contains a list of currently active transactions (Active Transaction List) along with a horizon defining transaction IDs that are globally committed."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"bool HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC(HeapTupleHeader tuple, Snapshot snapshot) {
    if (!HeapTupleHeaderXminCommitted(tuple)) {
        if (TransactionIdIsCurrentTransactionId(tuple->t_xmin))
            return !HeapTupleHeaderXminInvalid(tuple);
        // Check against active snapshot list
        return false;
    }
    // Further checks for xmax...
    return true;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Garbage Collection and Vacuuming"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because MVCC generates multiple versions for updates and deletes, tables accumulate dead tuples (tuples no longer visible to any active or future transaction). Database systems require a background maintenance process, commonly known as a Vacuum or Garbage Collector, to reclaim this storage space."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The vacuum process scans tables to identify dead tuples whose xmax is older than the oldest active transaction snapshot."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It marks the space occupied by these dead tuples as reusable for future insertions."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding MVCC internals is critical for optimizing high-throughput database systems. By balancing version creation, snapshot isolation, and aggressive garbage collection, modern databases achieve high concurrency without sacrificing data integrity."}]
